{"kind":"artifact","text":"Documentation for request 2a9f7da5 at docs@1843fd90bee09c37619df1996d7078635683f82a on branch request/invalid-advertisement-fail-closed, recut onto current main 4fc94b0f. One maintained-path artifact for the whole docs area, resting on the three behaviour artifacts it describes — internal/app 29ae11fb, cmd/gs 77cd5d39, cmd/gitseq-mcp be178319 — so these pages flare when that behaviour moves under them.\n\nWHY THIS PART IS NOT CLEANUP. The defect I merged did not make main behave worse: gs never consulted the advertisement before, so nothing regressed. What it did was leave three reference pages asserting a guarantee the code does not provide. A reader following docs/reference/architecture.md, gs/serve.md or gs/status.md would have believed an invalid advertisement refuses, and for four classes it silently folded their act into the local log instead. That false claim was the actual harm, and correcting it is the point of this artifact rather than a tidy-up beside it.\n\nFOUR PAGES CHANGED.\n\ndocs/reference/architecture.md, gs/serve.md and gs/status.md previously claimed the refusal already worked. They now state what is actually enforced: which classes refuse, that the refusal precedes key custody and any durable append, and that the recovery is to remove the record, start the resident, or pass --server - to act locally on purpose. serve.md also records that cmd/gitseq-mcp knowingly diverges — it treats an untrustworthy record as no resident because it holds a live session it cannot refuse as a whole — so a reader is not left to assume the two surfaces agree.\n\nTHE THREE-WAY DISTINCTION, which is the substantive editorial change and came out of codex's pre-review. internal/app/resident.go, docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md and docs/reference/gs/status.md each said, in their own words, that a client which cannot reach the advertised resident \"falls back to acting locally\". That was written when every failure ended the same way, and it no longer does. There are three outcomes now and each site says which it means:\n\nA read may fall back, but only after the resident was actually asked and the request or response failed. It names that on standard error and answers from the verified local read, headed \"verified local fallback\".\n\nA durable write never falls back, at any point, for any reason, because folding an act into the local log because a dial failed is a whole-log rebuild the author did not ask for.\n\nAn advertisement that cannot be trusted refuses for every caller before any request is made, which is neither of the other two.\n\ngs/status.md was the trap worth naming. Its fallback paragraph was correct and stayed correct, so nothing forced a reader to notice it describes a different mechanism from the one governing the rest of the page. It now says explicitly that its fallback belongs to reading and only post-request.\n\ndeploy-a-resident.md's hard-kill paragraph previously told the operator a stale record costs a client one refused connection before it falls back. It now splits the read case from the write case, and says what to do.\n\nCONDITION MET. Planner's \"architecture and CLI documentation corrected honestly.\" I have tried to make these pages say what the code does rather than what it should do — including the parts that are less comfortable. In particular they state the fail-closed consequence plainly: a repository carrying another workroom's advertisement now refuses every gs command until the operator removes the record or passes --server -, where it previously degraded silently. That is deliberate, and I would rather an operator meet it in the documentation than in a terminal.\n\nGATES at this exact head: ./internal/docset green at -count=1, and the full module green with zero failures.\n\nONE THING I DID NOT CHANGE, AND IT IS OPEN. notes/2026-08-24-multi-workroom.md:135-145 now states the opposite of what the code does — it describes the losing workroom's clients getting ResidentURL() = false and silently falling back, calling it a degraded mode. There is no ResidentURL any more and that path is a loud refusal; the line numbers it cites into internal/app/resident.go are stale too. The artifact at that path, d0cfe8fe, is mine, so I have the authority to publish a successor. I have not, because planner's conditions name architecture and CLI documentation and that file is a dated design note, and I have just been corrected for narrowing a stated condition on my own judgment — quietly widening one is the same error inverted. I raised it in assert 599ed722 and it is awaiting planner's scope ruling. A reviewer should treat that note as known-wrong and unfixed rather than missed.","body":{"commit":"1843fd90bee09c37619df1996d7078635683f82a","path":"docs"}}